Looking at this problem, I need to find the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $21^n \equiv 1 \pmod{10^{2015}}$, i.e., the multiplicative order of 21 modulo $10^{2015}$.

## Setting up via CRT

Since $10^{2015} = 2^{2015} \cdot 5^{2015}$ and $\gcd(2^{2015}, 5^{2015}) = 1$:

$$\text{ord}_{10^{2015}}(21) = \text{lcm}\!\left(\text{ord}_{2^{2015}}(21),\ \text{ord}_{5^{2015}}(21)\right).$$

Note $\gcd(21, 10) = 1$ since $21 = 3 \cdot 7$.

## Order modulo $2^{2015}$

For $k \ge 3$, the group $(\mathbb{Z}/2^k\mathbb{Z})^* \cong \mathbb{Z}/2 \times \mathbb{Z}/2^{k-2}$, generated by $-1$ and $5$, where $5$ has order $2^{k-2}$.

Since $21 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, the element $21$ lies in the cyclic subgroup $\langle 5 \rangle$, so $21 \equiv 5^j \pmod{2^k}$ for some $j$.

Computing mod 16: $5^1 \equiv 5$, $5^2 \equiv 9$, $5^3 \equiv 13$, $5^4 \equiv 1$. Since $21 \equiv 5 \pmod{16}$, we get $j \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, so **$j$ is odd**.

The order of $5^j$ mod $2^k$ is $\dfrac{2^{k-2}}{\gcd(j,\, 2^{k-2})}$. Since $j$ is odd, $\gcd(j, 2^{k-2}) = 1$, giving:

$$\text{ord}_{2^{2015}}(21) = 2^{2015-2} = 2^{2013}.$$

**Verification at small cases:** mod 8, $21 \equiv 5$, $5^2 \equiv 1$, order $2 = 2^{3-2}$ ✓. Mod 32, $5^5 \equiv 21$ with $\gcd(5,8)=1$, order $8 = 2^{5-2}$ ✓. Mod 64, $5^{13} \equiv 21$ with $\gcd(13,16)=1$, order $16 = 2^{6-2}$ ✓.

## Order modulo $5^{2015}$

Here $21 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$, and $21 - 1 = 20 = 4 \cdot 5$, so $v_5(21 - 1) = 1$ (i.e., $21 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$ but $21 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{25}$).

By the standard lifting lemma for odd primes: if $a = 1 + cp$ with $p \nmid c$, then $\text{ord}_{p^k}(a) = p^{k-1}$ for all $k \ge 1$.

Here $c = 4$ and $5 \nmid 4$, so:

$$\text{ord}_{5^{2015}}(21) = 5^{2015-1} = 5^{2014}.$$

## Combining

Since $2^{2013}$ and $5^{2014}$ are coprime:

$$n = \text{lcm}(2^{2013},\, 5^{2014}) = 2^{2013} \cdot 5^{2014}.$$

## Number of positive divisors

The prime factorization is $n = 2^{2013} \cdot 5^{2014}$, so:

$$\tau(n) = (2013 + 1)(2014 + 1) = 2014 \cdot 2015.$$

Computing:
$$2014 \cdot 2015 = 2014 \cdot 2000 + 2014 \cdot 15 = 4{,}028{,}000 + 30{,}210 = 4{,}058{,}210.$$

Taking remainder mod 1000:

$$4{,}058{,}210 \pmod{1000} = 210.$$

## Answer

$$\boxed{210}$$

